WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.880
You become the kind of amalgamation of the five people that you spend the most time with.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:08.200
Let's say you meet a person and you're like, I want this person to be in my circle.

3
00:00:09.060 --> 00:00:13.320
How do you approach this person if you guys are not besties already?

4
00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:17.000
When you're trying to find people that you want to work with, you try to find...

5
00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:22.740
You think your money's gone and never getting it back, that's totally unacceptable and people get really angry at you.

6
00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.820
This is now the cycle of delivery.

7
00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:32.020
And I think a lot of kind of the white paper hype and then the projects die type thing or they can never be viable businesses type thing.

8
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:33.400
I think it's mostly dead.

9
00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:34.820
Bitcoin's at the center of it.

10
00:00:34.960 --> 00:00:36.540
You're using it to go get stables.

11
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.500
It can look like a neobank and now your Bitcoin is earning interest as well.

12
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:42.460
If you don't know what the yield is, you're the yield.

13
00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:46.820
How many markets that are relatively untapped are a trillion dollars in size today?

14
00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:47.880
There's not too many.

15
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:52.040
And that's just such an exciting thing to really try to capture.

16
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:53.980
When are we going to get a million dollar Bitcoin now?

17
00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:55.460
It's like, well, I hope it's not this year.

18
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:59.500
Because if it's this year, we'll change the Bitcoin market forever.

19
00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:16.700
Hi, Rich.

20
00:01:17.720 --> 00:01:18.980
Hey, great to chat, Bonnie.

21
00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:19.520
Excited.

22
00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:29.140
You said in one of your interviews that you are the zero to one guy.

23
00:01:29.140 --> 00:01:32.740
And, you know, you started your first company at 18 years old.

24
00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:38.100
Can you share more about your mindset and what inspired you to do all these things?

25
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.720
I've been told by a bunch of people I have a very high figure it out factor.

26
00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.400
And I think I've had that since I was a kid.

27
00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:48.360
You know, leave me in a room with a bunch of random toys or whatever, and I'm going to figure something out.

28
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.520
And that's just kind of how my brain works.

29
00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:55.820
And kind of the corresponding element to that is when things are, like, totally figured out, I tend to lose interest.

30
00:01:56.580 --> 00:02:00.900
And that's one of the reasons that I love crypto, because it's always problem solving, right?

31
00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:06.100
And, like, you're always trying to work on some new narrative or some new technological piece for the platform.

32
00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:08.560
Like, there's kind of a never-ending problem, if you will.

33
00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:11.740
And that is just so conducive with how my brain works.

34
00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:13.960
And it's been like that since a really, really young age.

35
00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:23.980
And my parents were so great at giving me the amount of freedom with which to go explore my own interests without being intrusive, if you will.

36
00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.240
And that allowed me to go into so many different avenues.

37
00:02:27.620 --> 00:02:35.200
And I don't know if I've ever told this story in a podcast, but, like, how I actually got into coding back in the day was actually I spent a summer just reading books.

38
00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:38.960
Just like my parents said, hey, you know, you don't need to get a summer job, whatever, just go read.

39
00:02:39.080 --> 00:02:44.100
But I found programming, eventually wrote my first iPhone apps, and, you know, kind of the history is there.

40
00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:50.800
It was a lot easier to make iPhone apps back in the day because there was, like, a thousand apps in the store, as I make myself, you know, old on this podcast.

41
00:02:51.580 --> 00:02:57.260
But, yeah, it was a lot of fun, and that sort of creative freedom was definitely very key in shaping my life.

42
00:02:58.460 --> 00:03:00.000
Who taught you how to code?

43
00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.520
So I'm totally self-taught.

44
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:02.640
Wow.

45
00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:06.740
I attempted to learn in college where I didn't fully stick.

46
00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:13.100
So then I actually found on my own, and then all of a sudden, you know, my brain just kind of clicked with how I think about things.

47
00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.180
But kind of the traditional learning style was never really my thing.

48
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.160
I'm a much more experiential person, and that's where the Internet was just so important.

49
00:03:20.220 --> 00:03:21.020
You can just go learn anything.

50
00:03:21.260 --> 00:03:24.220
And, again, it was an amazing time to go put those skills to use.

51
00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:27.180
And you are a serial entrepreneur.

52
00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:30.780
So what are some businesses you've been a part of?

53
00:03:30.780 --> 00:03:32.540
Tell us these experiences.

54
00:03:33.500 --> 00:03:41.180
So shortly after college, I cut my teeth out in San Francisco, and we had a number of, like, you know, random kind of businesses in college.

55
00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:41.920
It never really went anywhere.

56
00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:43.940
They were great, formative learning experiences.

57
00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:46.700
And we did that when we were really young, you know, 17, 18 years old.

58
00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:53.480
Got to meet with so many amazing entrepreneurs in Boston and really kind of see how the sausage is made with a lot of these different businesses.

59
00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.600
And you learn so many of those lessons, like, experientially.

60
00:03:56.860 --> 00:04:00.020
It's so hard to, like, read a textbook on startups or something, right?

61
00:04:00.040 --> 00:04:01.820
Like, you have to go chew glass.

62
00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:03.180
You have to go learn these things yourself.

63
00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:12.320
So by the time we made it out to San Francisco in, you know, our early 20s, we had seen a lot of what the startup life had to offer, but not kind of the full picture.

64
00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:19.980
And I was fortunate enough to go work at some amazing companies as an employee initially to then go learn, okay, this is how you scale businesses.

65
00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:23.060
This is what it takes to, you know, go from a Series A to Series D company.

66
00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.360
Like, we got to see so much of that firsthand.

67
00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.080
And by being an engineer, it was getting my hands, how do you build things fast?

68
00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:31.600
How do you actually scale these tech platforms?

69
00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:34.100
And, again, there was just so much learning so fast.

70
00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:39.080
And then from there, parlayed it into several of my first entrepreneurial journeys were in the SaaS space.

71
00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:49.960
So one of which that I don't know if I've talked about prior, but it's actually, like, in the weeds call center software, which is, like, really gross checkerboard style SaaS, you know?

72
00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:55.260
And that was a great place to learn because you're selling essentially perfectly competitive products.

73
00:04:55.680 --> 00:04:57.600
So you can innovate on the edges.

74
00:04:57.800 --> 00:04:58.640
You can build new features.

75
00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:00.220
You can have better user experiences.

76
00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:05.000
But at the end of the day, you're trying to sell to very sophisticated buyers that have very established buying criteria.

77
00:05:05.360 --> 00:05:12.360
And doing that when you're really young, selling to, you know, large, you know, organizations is quite difficult, especially if you're trying to sell enterprise deals.

78
00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:18.940
But it taught me so much about not only just software engineering and building SaaS products at scale, but also how do you sell?

79
00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:23.940
And I think the killer combination for most folks is being able to either sell or build.

80
00:05:23.940 --> 00:05:26.700
And I'm very fortunate to be able to be very good at both.

81
00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:33.040
And I think that's kind of my unique combination and what I bring as, you know, kind of a leader of crypto projects today.

82
00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:37.920
But I do think you become the kind of amalgamation of the five people that you spend the most time with.

83
00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:50.680
And I've been really fortunate over the last couple decades to constantly be refining that set to have, you know, five to 10 people who are, you know, movers and shakers of, you know, real amazing businesses, not just in crypto, but in Web2.

84
00:05:50.920 --> 00:05:52.620
And that's formed a lot of who I am today.

85
00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.940
So let's say you meet a person and you're like, I want this person to be in my circle.

86
00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.240
How do you approach this person if you guys are not besties already?

87
00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:07.140
So I've always been a believer in the pay it forward culture.

88
00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:10.440
And this is something that was ingrained to me at a very early age.

89
00:06:10.560 --> 00:06:15.340
But when you're trying to, you know, find people that you want to work with, you try to find how can I help you?

90
00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:19.300
And then in theory, maybe some of that will eventually come back around to you.

91
00:06:19.340 --> 00:06:22.000
But I actually don't really believe in transactional relationships.

92
00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:33.700
I think too much of crypto in particular is transactional, where people try to set up things where it's unfortunately a lot of like heads I win, tails you lose type scenarios versus, hey, how do we go win-win together?

93
00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:41.260
And I think by approaching it with an open mind and to go try to find things that you can work on together or things you might be able to do for other people, it comes back around.

94
00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:48.060
And I've been very fortunate with a lot of these relationships where you start doing something small together, you might help someone in some small way.

95
00:06:48.060 --> 00:06:52.980
And then in the future, you know, you know exactly who to call for, you know, some sort of thing down the line.

96
00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:55.200
And it's amazing to have built that rapport.

97
00:06:56.100 --> 00:07:01.800
Okay. And that kind of aligns with this DAO kind of mentality. Is that correct?

98
00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:04.580
I'd say it does and it doesn't.

99
00:07:04.720 --> 00:07:09.680
I think in general, like the DAO is less about paying it forward.

100
00:07:09.680 --> 00:07:15.940
It's more just new collaboration models, if you will, which you wind up being very collaborative in that way.

101
00:07:15.940 --> 00:07:18.240
But it's not kind of as explicit, if you will.

102
00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:25.540
DAO at the end of the day has a very specific purpose, which is to grow the core network, to scale Bitcoin, like very kind of clearly defined.

103
00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:29.200
I think in a lot of these personal relationships, you actually don't know where it's going to go.

104
00:07:29.800 --> 00:07:34.560
You know, like you might meet someone, you know, at some point or, you know, at some event or whatever very early.

105
00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:38.520
And then they might be doing something totally different, you know, five, 10 years from now.

106
00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:40.720
And that's actually, it's really funny.

107
00:07:40.720 --> 00:07:44.800
One of the folks that, you know, core partner with very early is a group called Switchboard.

108
00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:46.120
Switchboard's an Oracle.

109
00:07:46.380 --> 00:07:47.960
They're live on Core, live in Solana.

110
00:07:48.080 --> 00:07:49.040
They've been around for a while.

111
00:07:49.180 --> 00:07:52.240
They're actually gearing up for their TGE, which, of course, we hope goes well.

112
00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:57.840
But I met Chris back in 2010, I think, at our time during Boston College.

113
00:07:58.260 --> 00:08:02.260
And we didn't reconnect until 2017 in a crypto context.

114
00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:04.940
So you never really know where these things will come from.

115
00:08:05.320 --> 00:08:07.480
But it's so powerful to build these long-term relationships.

116
00:08:07.480 --> 00:08:12.980
You also worked at Coinbase as a money movement engineer.

117
00:08:13.160 --> 00:08:20.860
Exchanges like Coinbase process, you know, up to trillions of dollars in in and out flows throughout a, you know, a given year.

118
00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:28.880
And that's the total dollarized volume of all the crypto assets, all of the fiat, because there's so many different payment rails, so many different crypto assets.

119
00:08:28.880 --> 00:08:32.520
And that's very hard to do reliably at scale.

120
00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:39.420
Having been a customer of a centralized exchange, I'd assume at some point in the past, you've probably had that dreaded, where is my money feeling?

121
00:08:40.120 --> 00:08:41.580
And it's always confusing.

122
00:08:41.620 --> 00:08:45.000
Even if it only takes another minute or two longer, it's scary, right?

123
00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:47.260
Because crypto, there's no takebacks, right?

124
00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:48.500
These are bare assets.

125
00:08:48.500 --> 00:08:53.840
And I had the fortunate spot for about four years to run all the money movements engineering at Coinbase.

126
00:08:54.180 --> 00:08:57.100
And it was a really amazing position to be in.

127
00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:02.120
I got to work with so many awesome people, you know, go through the company's initial direct listing, et cetera.

128
00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:08.640
But it taught me a lot about the, let's call it the next level craft of software engineering and doing things at incredibly high scale.

129
00:09:08.640 --> 00:09:14.580
So if you're at the volume of a Coinbase or a Binance, like the dollarized volumes are really impressive.

130
00:09:15.060 --> 00:09:19.260
And small problems can be very expensive problems very, very quickly.

131
00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:31.380
And there's a customer service element of that that I was also taught, you know, very clearly, where in some of my other roles, hey, maybe it's okay if someone can't, you know, access some SaaS tool for, you know, like an hour or something.

132
00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:35.660
Well, if you think your money's gone and never getting it back, that's totally unacceptable.

133
00:09:35.660 --> 00:09:38.420
And people get really angry at you, and rightfully so.

134
00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:52.980
So it gave me kind of this commitment to quality, commitment to excellence that I had prior, but it really sharpened it where when your team makes a mistake or something and your front page news on MSNBC, you know, the stakes are a little different, but I enjoyed it.

135
00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:54.760
It was like kind of a crypto finishing school.

136
00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:58.760
What I would say is kind of true to my earlier answer on being a zero to one guy.

137
00:09:58.880 --> 00:10:07.440
I enjoyed, you know, some of the earlier times at Coinbase, but as it got, you know, a little more corporate, that sort of thing, that's when, you know, it was time for me to find my next venture.

138
00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:15.360
Take us back to the moment when you discovered Bitcoin or the moment when you're like, hey, I can make this into a full-time job.

139
00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:20.720
So I first heard about Bitcoin, I believe in 2011 from a college professor.

140
00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:24.000
And the first time I heard about it, it made no sense to me.

141
00:10:24.320 --> 00:10:27.920
It was in the context of computer security.

142
00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:32.960
And he was explaining solving the visiting general's problem in like a pretty academic level, if you would.

143
00:10:33.480 --> 00:10:34.580
And I was like, that's really interesting.

144
00:10:34.940 --> 00:10:36.300
No one's ever going to use money on the Internet.

145
00:10:36.300 --> 00:10:41.800
Like, that doesn't make any sense, you know, like a true, like, you know, hardheaded kind of college student, you know, type view.

146
00:10:42.400 --> 00:10:47.880
By 2013, I had really sat down to read the Bitcoin white paper again, you know, more in detail.

147
00:10:48.180 --> 00:10:55.660
And then it really clicked for me because it was, hey, how do you build an alternative to traditional financial system, a non-sovereign store of value?

148
00:10:55.660 --> 00:11:02.620
Now I started to get it because it was this, like, economic play on top of this crypto security play.

149
00:11:02.700 --> 00:11:04.520
All these different pieces were all kind of aligning.

150
00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:10.600
And that to me was where I was like, okay, this could potentially be something that totally changes the world.

151
00:11:10.700 --> 00:11:12.640
And at that point, Bitcoin was, you know, popular.

152
00:11:12.760 --> 00:11:13.500
We're talking about it.

153
00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:16.780
It was nowhere near the size or importance that it is today on a global scale.

154
00:11:17.620 --> 00:11:24.680
And that's when I, you know, decided, hey, this is something I want to put, you know, my future career aspirations towards.

155
00:11:24.680 --> 00:11:28.660
And we just need to figure out kind of the right modality with which to do it.

156
00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:41.100
And then by the time Ethereum started to get popular, really, in like 2015-ish, 2014, 2015-ish, that's when the second leg of the understanding really clicked, which was, oh, now you can have programmable money, right?

157
00:11:41.140 --> 00:11:44.420
Like, it's not just, you know, kind of this value that you're sitting around.

158
00:11:44.520 --> 00:11:46.120
You can actually do full smart contracts.

159
00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:47.260
You can have tokens.

160
00:11:47.480 --> 00:11:49.760
Like, all these sort of pieces started to come in together.

161
00:11:50.020 --> 00:11:53.620
And then by the 2017 cycle, I was like, this is what I'm going to spend the rest of my life on.

162
00:11:54.680 --> 00:12:02.600
So it's kind of this crawl, walk, run from like the early Bitcoin, like kind of baptism all the way through kind of understanding all that Ethereum was.

163
00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:08.520
And then now today being back in Bitcoin land, which, you know, it's awesome to have come home to where it all started.

164
00:12:08.780 --> 00:12:13.880
You know how Bitcoiners, they, a lot of them do not like the other altcoins.

165
00:12:14.380 --> 00:12:15.220
What are you like?

166
00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:19.320
So I was definitely a Bitcoin maxi back in the day.

167
00:12:20.220 --> 00:12:29.080
I think a lot of folks kind of go through some sort of a spectrum where they like start out Bitcoin maxi, then they become like kind of an altcoin everywhere maxi, and then they kind of go back.

168
00:12:29.080 --> 00:12:36.840
And it's a kind of a common thing where you get burned by so many of these things in crypto that just over promise and under deliver.

169
00:12:37.380 --> 00:12:47.180
And if we've seen that in so many other cycles, my view is more that there's a lot of truly amazing crypto projects out there and that this is now the cycle of delivery.

170
00:12:47.180 --> 00:12:59.060
And I think a lot of kind of a white paper hype and then the projects die type thing, or they can never be viable businesses type thing, I think is mostly dead, which is, I think, a good meta for to die out in general.

171
00:12:59.520 --> 00:13:03.400
And I think we should be looking at can these be revenue generating platform businesses.

172
00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:12.420
And the analogy that I give a lot of people, which is unsexy, if you will, but a lot of the business of like blockchain, at least from my point of view, looks quite similar to Salesforce.

173
00:13:13.120 --> 00:13:14.540
And you're building a platform.

174
00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:16.320
It's purely usage-based pricing.

175
00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:17.520
Those are transaction fees.

176
00:13:17.920 --> 00:13:19.740
You're trying to attract a lot of developers.

177
00:13:20.000 --> 00:13:21.520
You also attract end users.

178
00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:24.800
You take rev shares in the form of these transaction fees back and forth.

179
00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:28.400
And it's a pretty simple business when you think about it at the end of the day.

180
00:13:28.500 --> 00:13:34.720
But if you can scale these, they're essentially zero marginal cost businesses that should be spitting off quite a bit of free cash flow.

181
00:13:35.200 --> 00:13:42.580
And that's what I'm excited about is this product market fit, this next level of maturity in the blockchain space where some of these ecosystems, we've already seen it.

182
00:13:42.580 --> 00:13:48.320
Look at Solana, look at Ethereum, these generates real cash flows, even by public company standards.

183
00:13:48.960 --> 00:13:54.460
And that's something that Core hopes to do in its journey as well and something that we're definitely focused on doing.

184
00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:56.560
Now, tell us about Core then.

185
00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:59.980
So Core is the leading Bitcoin scaling solution.

186
00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:07.320
So it's got the largest Bitcoin DeFi ecosystem on one side with about a billion in TVL, hundreds of thousands of daily active users.

187
00:14:07.840 --> 00:14:12.480
It's got 75% of the Bitcoin hash rate that's currently securing the Core network.

188
00:14:12.480 --> 00:14:19.880
And then it also has the largest real yield Bitcoin staking product with about 800 million in Bitcoin that's earning passive yield.

189
00:14:20.440 --> 00:14:24.200
And the way to think about Core is it's really the Bitcoin consumer chain, right?

190
00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:26.980
It's the place where you can go put your Bitcoin to work on one side.

191
00:14:27.200 --> 00:14:32.020
And that can be in full Bitcoin DeFi or in these more passive products like Bitcoin staking.

192
00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:37.300
And the idea is to have a product for at every stage of a Bitcoiner's journey, right?

193
00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:40.400
Some folks are ready to go into full Bitcoin DeFi today.

194
00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:43.440
The majority are maybe ready for Bitcoin staking, right?

195
00:14:43.520 --> 00:14:45.300
Different trust assumptions, different risk thresholds.

196
00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:50.960
But it's so important to meet these folks where they're at today versus to force them into things they're not ready for.

197
00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:53.960
Because this is truly a multi-decade play.

198
00:14:54.200 --> 00:15:06.060
And the example that I tell most folks is if you look at the Ethereum comps relative to the Bitcoin comps, even just looking at the DeFi aspect, there should be 200 billion of Bitcoin that's in DeFi today.

199
00:15:06.060 --> 00:15:08.280
And we're not even remotely close to that.

200
00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:12.080
So let's say there's a 10x at least there, you know, short term.

201
00:15:12.500 --> 00:15:17.560
Well, if you now say that I think Bitcoin is going to be worth maybe 10 plus trillion in the next five years.

202
00:15:18.140 --> 00:15:20.540
Okay, now you're talking a trillion dollar space.

203
00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:25.080
How many markets that are relatively untapped are a trillion dollars in size today?

204
00:15:25.160 --> 00:15:26.160
There's not too many.

205
00:15:26.600 --> 00:15:30.340
And that's just such an exciting thing to really try to capture.

206
00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:34.520
And that's what Core is after by, you know, building the Bitcoin consumer chain.

207
00:15:34.520 --> 00:15:37.380
Can you explain what trust assumption is?

208
00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:38.080
Correct.

209
00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:44.680
So the trust assumptions are what you're willing to trust in order to achieve some end.

210
00:15:44.820 --> 00:15:48.420
And I hate to use definitions as like how to explain it.

211
00:15:48.420 --> 00:15:55.980
But I think we can go through an example here and we'll use Core's Bitcoin staking analogy to talk about it.

212
00:15:56.400 --> 00:16:09.580
So when you stake your Bitcoin in Core's product, you just time lock it to yourself using Bitcoin's native time lock functionality, which just locks up those funds from a technical level.

213
00:16:09.980 --> 00:16:12.140
You're just time locking a UTXO to yourself.

214
00:16:12.480 --> 00:16:15.140
You're never sending it to any multi-sig, etc.

215
00:16:15.140 --> 00:16:17.140
Now, what does that mean from a trust assumption point of view?

216
00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:19.420
It means you only have to trust Bitcoin.

217
00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:24.680
So you don't even have to trust Core because you're only trusting the native pieces of Bitcoin itself.

218
00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:31.480
Let's talk about maybe a different way to implement Bitcoin staking, which would be, let's call it via like a multi-sig or something else.

219
00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:35.360
Well, maybe for that, you actually have to send your Bitcoin somewhere else.

220
00:16:36.000 --> 00:16:38.340
Maybe even a better example would be like lending, right?

221
00:16:38.340 --> 00:16:42.180
Where you have to send it somewhere else and then they promise to pay you yield in the future.

222
00:16:42.780 --> 00:16:48.000
Well, now you have to trust that those people will actually give you your Bitcoin back in the future, right?

223
00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:49.980
That's the trust assumption that you're taking on.

224
00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:55.480
So the way to think about it, I guess, maybe in a more like accounting sense is like the accrued liabilities, right?

225
00:16:55.480 --> 00:17:04.800
And it's with each one of those, you have to be really certain that you're willing to exchange your Bitcoin or your crypto asset for whatever they're going to promise you on the other side.

226
00:17:05.340 --> 00:17:09.740
And just for me, having been in the crypto space for a long time, I don't like to trust.

227
00:17:10.100 --> 00:17:10.980
I like to verify.

228
00:17:10.980 --> 00:17:21.660
And I would never put my own Bitcoin in some of these other products where I have to trust the third party because that, to me, is just something that's an unacceptable risk no matter what they're paying me.

229
00:17:22.020 --> 00:17:25.600
But for other people that might be even farther out in the risk spectrum, they're willing to do so.

230
00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:28.460
But the key is to do what's right for you and where you're at.

231
00:17:28.460 --> 00:17:41.120
And I think there's a nice competitive pressure that Core is helping to bring on the space today, which is trying to reduce as many trust assumptions as possible, not just with staking, but with things like bridging as well.

232
00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:41.920
That's very clear.

233
00:17:42.320 --> 00:17:48.100
That's why you said it's risk-free, because I've been watching your interviews and I was like, huh, what do you mean by risk-free?

234
00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:49.380
Okay, that makes sense now.

235
00:17:50.420 --> 00:17:53.760
Would you compare that to, let's say, Lido, Ethereum?

236
00:17:54.440 --> 00:17:55.620
What would that look like?

237
00:17:55.620 --> 00:18:02.260
In the Lido case, you're actually trusting an operator in order to go do that stake for you.

238
00:18:02.900 --> 00:18:05.760
And again, that's not necessarily a bad thing.

239
00:18:05.940 --> 00:18:09.100
Lido has a really amazing reputation.

240
00:18:09.340 --> 00:18:11.620
There's a lot of social consensus that goes around that.

241
00:18:11.880 --> 00:18:14.100
They have an amazing operating history.

242
00:18:14.620 --> 00:18:18.520
But there is some sort of a trust that you're putting in another group.

243
00:18:18.860 --> 00:18:20.240
And that's not necessarily a bad thing.

244
00:18:20.500 --> 00:18:22.860
But it's just something that people should think about.

245
00:18:22.860 --> 00:18:34.080
And I think an example of kind of how that plays out in the core system, if you will, is there's a lot of Bitcoin LSTs that are now becoming quite popular.

246
00:18:34.500 --> 00:18:39.020
And core actually has its own LST BTC that will come out here relatively soon.

247
00:18:39.580 --> 00:18:46.060
And the idea is now for that, okay, you want to go take your yield-bearing asset and you want to go bring that into DeFi.

248
00:18:46.060 --> 00:18:55.060
Well, in order to do that, you have to take on other trust assumptions because now there needs to be a derivative token that gets minted out of that.

249
00:18:55.280 --> 00:19:02.920
So you're trading off either custody with a multi-sig or like a tri-party or like MPC, et cetera, right?

250
00:19:03.340 --> 00:19:06.240
And with that, you're getting some additional utility out of it.

251
00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:13.900
And I think that's kind of how to think about, again, like when you checkerboard these different things to go back to what I was talking in the beginning, am I getting what I desire?

252
00:19:14.100 --> 00:19:17.380
And am I willing to accept the different risks that come with it?

253
00:19:17.720 --> 00:19:20.160
And for a lot of people, it just wants to stake.

254
00:19:20.440 --> 00:19:22.740
So it's amazing that you can go do that with no new trust assumptions.

255
00:19:23.220 --> 00:19:30.640
If you want to go further out of the yield curve, you want to go out there and get higher things, you're probably, at least today, going to have to go take on some new trust assumptions to go do so.

256
00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:33.680
Okay, another question on time lock.

257
00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.940
So again, for newbies, what does that mean?

258
00:19:37.360 --> 00:19:42.120
There's a Bitcoin opcode called check lock time verify.

259
00:19:42.480 --> 00:19:44.840
I believe it was introduced in 2015.

260
00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:48.840
So it's quite, you know, it's quite ossified at this point.

261
00:19:48.940 --> 00:19:51.080
It's, you know, it's battle tested and that sort of thing.

262
00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:53.020
And it's a very basic primitive.

263
00:19:53.020 --> 00:19:56.480
Like if you think about the scripting language of Bitcoin, it's very basic.

264
00:19:56.880 --> 00:20:00.540
You can only do kind of these small things, but there's enough primitives to be dangerous.

265
00:20:00.640 --> 00:20:01.160
If you will.

266
00:20:01.280 --> 00:20:10.760
And there's this whole, you know, kind of world of Bitcoin script and these amazing engineers that are constantly figuring out how to expand the potential of what you can do with Bitcoin script.

267
00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:12.140
Despite it being so limited.

268
00:20:12.520 --> 00:20:16.340
I think one of the great examples of that is all the amazing work being done like the BitVM space, right?

269
00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:20.900
Where it's like very limited, but you can actually actually build amazing pieces out of it.

270
00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:27.300
So one of these basic primitives, again, not related to BitVM stuff, but relevant to this context is the ability to lock your Bitcoin.

271
00:20:27.640 --> 00:20:31.860
And that allows you to do like basic smart contracts to a degree, if you will, right?

272
00:20:31.960 --> 00:20:38.120
And this basic contract is, okay, I set a block time in the future, a block number in the future.

273
00:20:38.120 --> 00:20:43.180
And up until that point, that UTXO cannot be spent.

274
00:20:43.940 --> 00:20:48.100
And it essentially just prevents it from moving is kind of the way to think about it.

275
00:20:48.220 --> 00:20:51.040
And that's what you're doing as part of the staking pieces.

276
00:20:51.040 --> 00:20:54.440
You're actually like encumbering those funds for some period of time.

277
00:20:54.440 --> 00:21:04.680
But that's why we say you only have to trust Bitcoin, because if you trust Bitcoin to be able to do check, lock time, verify, that's the only piece of this that you have to trust working.

278
00:21:05.140 --> 00:21:11.860
There's no case where you're performing that and core or some other bad actor is taking your principle, right?

279
00:21:11.940 --> 00:21:20.500
And that's what most people, you know, historically have the issues with where, hey, there's some piece of this where my principle actually gets at risk.

280
00:21:20.500 --> 00:21:27.940
And for most Bitcoiners, that's a totally unacceptable tradeoff where they think Bitcoin is the most sacred, precious asset in the universe.

281
00:21:28.660 --> 00:21:28.780
Okay.

282
00:21:28.900 --> 00:21:34.420
So from your perspective, let's say I lock, time lock one BTC.

283
00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:39.000
And how does that benefit you if I don't send it to you?

284
00:21:39.120 --> 00:21:43.300
So you're providing economic security to the core chain.

285
00:21:43.380 --> 00:21:48.380
So the way to think about it, if we go into kind of the Satoshi Plus mechanism again.

286
00:21:48.380 --> 00:21:52.680
So on one side, you've got the hash rate that's delegated to you.

287
00:21:52.760 --> 00:21:55.760
On the other side, you've got the core that's being staked to you.

288
00:21:55.900 --> 00:21:59.120
And then in this case, you've got the Bitcoin that's being staked to you.

289
00:21:59.600 --> 00:22:06.220
So as a validator, the more Bitcoin that you get staked to you, the more likely you are to get elected.

290
00:22:06.220 --> 00:22:17.980
And then by having there be a large quantum of Bitcoin that is staked to you, that makes it harder and harder for other bad actors to get elected into your validator set.

291
00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:18.340
Right.

292
00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:32.220
And the idea is by having these three hard to get quantities, you make it difficult for these folks that want to perform a malicious double sign or other sort of attack on your system to be able to actually get elected to that validator set.

293
00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:34.080
And we have three ingredients.

294
00:22:34.340 --> 00:22:37.940
You can think about it in like your Ethereum space and your Solana, et cetera.

295
00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:38.280
Right.

296
00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:39.380
It's one ingredient.

297
00:22:39.620 --> 00:22:42.380
And again, it's not necessarily to say that three is greater than one or whatever.

298
00:22:42.380 --> 00:22:48.840
They're just different models, but it's kind of that same idea just applied to three different contexts, if you will.

299
00:22:49.600 --> 00:23:00.080
If I have my Bitcoin in a ledger or in, I don't know, decentralized wallet, how do I earn yield on core?

300
00:23:00.960 --> 00:23:02.840
Like what does it look like, the process?

301
00:23:03.440 --> 00:23:08.060
So several different ways that you can go about generating this transaction.

302
00:23:08.060 --> 00:23:15.160
So you can go to stake.cordow.org and on stake.cordow.org, there's like a whole kind of WYSIWYG UI.

303
00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:18.980
It shows you a calculator that shows you your staking rewards.

304
00:23:19.060 --> 00:23:21.340
And then it shows you how you can boost it with dual staking.

305
00:23:21.800 --> 00:23:26.100
So if you stake both Bitcoin and core, you earn much higher rates of return.

306
00:23:26.480 --> 00:23:29.520
And this is one of the utilities that core provides is yield on your Bitcoin.

307
00:23:29.520 --> 00:23:32.620
So it's a really nice kind of symbiosis to these two different pieces.

308
00:23:32.880 --> 00:23:40.740
And then you can use Unisat, X-First, et cetera, to go build that transaction and to go broadcast that onto the Bitcoin network.

309
00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:41.780
That's one way.

310
00:23:41.840 --> 00:23:44.200
And I'd say that that's the more common way that we see.

311
00:23:44.960 --> 00:23:47.320
And that's probably the more retail focused way, if you will.

312
00:23:47.500 --> 00:23:52.980
And there's a bunch of hardware wallets, et cetera, that are integrating core with Bitcoin staking to make that more seamless.

313
00:23:53.100 --> 00:23:54.800
We'll continue to do that throughout all of 2025.

314
00:23:55.700 --> 00:23:58.440
On the more institutional side, you've got a couple of different options.

315
00:23:58.440 --> 00:24:01.900
What people will do is they'll work with their favorite custodian.

316
00:24:02.160 --> 00:24:07.100
So Hextrust, BitGo, like Copper, all these different ones that support core today.

317
00:24:07.220 --> 00:24:08.940
And they make it really simple and easy to use.

318
00:24:09.340 --> 00:24:11.340
Or you can build your own transaction.

319
00:24:11.540 --> 00:24:17.080
And core has a whole script that you can follow and write your own, et cetera, to go build that and then broadcast it yourself.

320
00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:21.360
So you have, again, going down the list of things, who do you trust?

321
00:24:21.780 --> 00:24:23.020
Which one makes it easy?

322
00:24:23.160 --> 00:24:24.800
Do you have any cold storage or not?

323
00:24:24.880 --> 00:24:24.980
Right?

324
00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:27.980
Like you go through kind of these different things and ultimately pick what's right for you.

325
00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:31.760
But there's a simple way for everybody that matches their goals.

326
00:24:32.440 --> 00:24:38.380
So if you are a retail that, you know, likes Bitcoin, has a few, which one is the easiest?

327
00:24:38.780 --> 00:24:41.860
Easiest would be Unisad or Experts, I believe.

328
00:24:42.820 --> 00:24:45.200
They've got pretty good support for this stuff.

329
00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:48.660
So Bitcoin has been seen as a store of value.

330
00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.280
Do you think this view is a little outdated?

331
00:24:51.660 --> 00:24:55.640
Because BTC Phi is Bitcoin DeFi, right?

332
00:24:55.880 --> 00:24:58.220
So you actually have to put your Bitcoin to work.

333
00:24:59.060 --> 00:25:05.060
But, you know, you see Bitcoin as this digital goal that you don't want to, you know, show other people.

334
00:25:05.180 --> 00:25:06.560
You want to just keep it in a safe.

335
00:25:06.560 --> 00:25:09.540
Is that like a very outdated type of view?

336
00:25:09.800 --> 00:25:11.540
Or what do you think?

337
00:25:11.980 --> 00:25:13.900
So I don't think it's outdated.

338
00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:18.760
I think it's being updated slightly, if you will.

339
00:25:18.920 --> 00:25:23.780
So what I mean by that is it's the same store of value just applied in a new context.

340
00:25:23.780 --> 00:25:29.540
So if I go back to 2013, I thought we were going to use Bitcoin as a means of payment everywhere.

341
00:25:29.820 --> 00:25:32.340
And I bought a lot of very expensive coffees today.

342
00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:38.680
But what we saw throughout time is people don't really want to pay for things in volatile assets, right?

343
00:25:38.740 --> 00:25:40.640
Like there's just really not a lot of demand for it.

344
00:25:41.120 --> 00:25:44.920
Taxes aside, like you just don't want to pay for things that you think are going to go up in that current.

345
00:25:45.020 --> 00:25:46.640
Like it's just a bad idea for the most part.

346
00:25:47.260 --> 00:25:48.400
So what have we learned?

347
00:25:48.500 --> 00:25:52.500
We've learned that it's actually amazing as a store of value or collateral asset.

348
00:25:52.500 --> 00:25:55.440
And we're seeing this on the nation state level today, right?

349
00:25:55.500 --> 00:25:58.940
You're hearing Trump and some of these other groups talk about a strategic Bitcoin reserve.

350
00:25:59.200 --> 00:26:04.640
And they want to have that potentially to borrow against or to have financial products around.

351
00:26:05.000 --> 00:26:07.300
But it's ultimately being used as a source of collateral.

352
00:26:08.080 --> 00:26:13.560
And I think that's what we're going to see on the retail side as well, where you have your Bitcoin.

353
00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:17.340
What you want to ultimately do is bring that into faster, cheaper rails like Core.

354
00:26:17.460 --> 00:26:20.820
And then you want to borrow against it, likely for stable coins.

355
00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:23.720
So again, the killer use case is really leverage, if you will.

356
00:26:24.120 --> 00:26:28.340
Low LTV loans against your Bitcoins, pay your day-to-day bills, and then eventually pay it back.

357
00:26:28.940 --> 00:26:31.440
And I think that's how we actually realized the Bitcoin vision.

358
00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:35.300
And this is, again, what I've been thinking about for the last 11, 12 years now.

359
00:26:35.680 --> 00:26:38.840
But the whole idea was how do you build an alternative traditional financial system?

360
00:26:38.940 --> 00:26:40.800
Well, now we have the possibility to go do that.

361
00:26:41.280 --> 00:26:42.480
Bitcoin's at the center of it.

362
00:26:42.540 --> 00:26:44.180
You're using it to go get stables.

363
00:26:44.360 --> 00:26:45.540
It can look like a neobank.

364
00:26:45.540 --> 00:26:51.420
And now your Bitcoin is earning interest as well, right, in the form of this yield from Core is not custodial Bitcoin staking.

365
00:26:51.940 --> 00:26:53.660
So it's got all those pieces now.

366
00:26:53.840 --> 00:27:01.020
And the fact that you can do that at extremely fast, extremely cheap transactions on top of Core was kind of that other missing piece.

367
00:27:01.180 --> 00:27:05.440
Because the majority of the world is priced out of using Bitcoin, the base layer.

368
00:27:05.440 --> 00:27:12.200
And I think that's how we actually onboard, let's call it, you know, billions of people onto Bitcoin in the next, you know, 10 to 20 years.

369
00:27:12.480 --> 00:27:14.520
That's the shape of what it will look like.

370
00:27:14.820 --> 00:27:20.180
And you'll have a variety of other products that are maybe more Bitcoin DeFi, if you will, besides just lending and borrowing.

371
00:27:20.700 --> 00:27:24.920
That will be, you know, all sorts of different things that we know from the Ethereum ecosystem, et cetera.

372
00:27:24.920 --> 00:27:34.300
But some of these initial use cases can be hundreds of billions, like at size, just like unfathomable numbers, you know, at the size of Bitcoin DeFi today.

373
00:27:34.480 --> 00:27:37.020
But I think it looks small three to five years from now.

374
00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:39.700
The yield is in Core token.

375
00:27:39.860 --> 00:27:40.420
Is that correct?

376
00:27:41.060 --> 00:27:41.580
That's correct.

377
00:27:41.660 --> 00:27:41.760
Yeah.

378
00:27:41.800 --> 00:27:44.500
You just have to understand that the Core token, understand Core's yield.

379
00:27:44.740 --> 00:27:46.540
If you don't know what the yield is, you're the yield.

380
00:27:46.980 --> 00:27:47.420
Okay.

381
00:27:47.540 --> 00:27:51.300
And you have a limited supply of 2.1 billion.

382
00:27:51.300 --> 00:27:56.420
And I want to know what happens, you know, when all the tokens are mined.

383
00:27:57.320 --> 00:27:59.280
And how does that work afterwards?

384
00:27:59.800 --> 00:28:02.060
So Core is a fixed supply token.

385
00:28:02.540 --> 00:28:09.340
And that was very deliberate to, one, like kind of pay homage to Bitcoin, but also to give it more sustainable, you know, monetary policy.

386
00:28:09.800 --> 00:28:14.580
And one of the things that we believe is, you know, Bitcoin's monetary policy is so beautiful and so simple.

387
00:28:14.580 --> 00:28:22.180
It's just too extreme, if you will, for many of these use cases where it hard ratchets by cutting itself in half every four years.

388
00:28:22.340 --> 00:28:25.860
We get those minor security budget crisis things every few years.

389
00:28:25.860 --> 00:28:28.420
Like we've all heard that story, you know, many times now.

390
00:28:28.580 --> 00:28:30.680
And that was actually the original genesis of Core.

391
00:28:30.780 --> 00:28:33.920
It was to go be the second block reward for Bitcoin miners.

392
00:28:33.920 --> 00:28:39.200
And that's how 75% of the hash rate is currently secure in the Core network, because they get core block rewards.

393
00:28:39.300 --> 00:28:44.080
It helps them sustain, you know, their profitability and kind of smooth out that profitability curve.

394
00:28:44.760 --> 00:28:52.920
And with Bitcoin staking, we gave them another tool in their toolkit, which is, okay, now I can go stake my Bitcoin, stake my core, versus just sell my Bitcoin, sell my core.

395
00:28:52.920 --> 00:28:58.920
So you give the miners, you know, more and more freedom to kind of operate in the most efficient way possible for them.

396
00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:04.120
And retail also gets the benefit, as do institutions, from the same, you know, Bitcoin staking product.

397
00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:09.940
All of that preamble aside, Core has a 81-year emissions curve, so it's very flat.

398
00:29:10.360 --> 00:29:15.400
And the idea is that it's this long-term sustainable yield versus be something more like yield farming.

399
00:29:15.760 --> 00:29:21.440
So as more and more Bitcoin or Core get staked, there's no new tokens that get printed, like, more or less.

400
00:29:21.440 --> 00:29:23.780
It's a fixed amount that gets printed.

401
00:29:24.220 --> 00:29:28.660
So it's very sound from that point of view, and it decreases about, like, 3.6% a year or something.

402
00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:31.160
So it's, like, this very, you know, kind of gradual decrease.

403
00:29:31.700 --> 00:29:41.980
And the idea is that over time, this system moves more and more to being based off transaction fees, right, similar to what Bitcoin does, where the emissions decrease and more and more of the network is generating.

404
00:29:42.460 --> 00:29:51.340
And we do think over an 81-year time horizon, that will flip, and you will have, hopefully long before that, but you will actually have a higher reward being paid out just on the transaction fees.

405
00:29:51.340 --> 00:29:56.040
As time goes on, very similar to what Bitcoin looks like, but slightly tweaked.

