605
00:44:14.740 --> 00:44:17.700
But I think the scope of that is quite broad.

606
00:44:18.060 --> 00:44:19.540
And I think it's important to keep experimenting.

607
00:44:20.020 --> 00:44:24.400
Like, so much of crypto is, like, kind of throwing shit at the wall and seeing what sticks.

608
00:44:24.840 --> 00:44:29.060
And I think we're so early in the experimentation phase, not just for core, but all of crypto.

609
00:44:29.500 --> 00:44:32.720
That we really haven't seen too many of these breakout use cases beyond finance.

610
00:44:33.100 --> 00:44:35.380
That's an enormous space in amongst itself.

611
00:44:35.380 --> 00:44:37.660
But there's still so much left to be done there.

612
00:44:37.820 --> 00:44:42.280
And that's something that, you know, we encourage so many builders to keep experimenting and go find new things.

613
00:44:42.700 --> 00:44:46.760
And then the other side, core is also marketing, you know, super actively to developers.

614
00:44:46.940 --> 00:44:56.400
Because at the end of the day, the strength of your ecosystem, you need amazing developers that want to have coordinated projects, that want to build from the long term, and that critically want to build real businesses.

615
00:44:56.400 --> 00:45:01.180
So that same philosophy that we have at the platform level is the same thing we ask of our builders.

616
00:45:01.460 --> 00:45:06.040
If you're just kind of building something just to launch a token and be done, you're probably not a good fit for core.

617
00:45:06.260 --> 00:45:10.140
Because those businesses, I just don't think fundamentally have staying power.

618
00:45:10.140 --> 00:45:13.460
And I think, again, people are smart and kind of see through those things.

619
00:45:14.020 --> 00:45:17.820
And so many of these businesses can be cash flow positive really quickly.

620
00:45:18.320 --> 00:45:20.380
Like these actually can be durable businesses.

621
00:45:20.700 --> 00:45:25.460
And core has done some pretty innovative things with our ignition program that rewards users and builders.

622
00:45:25.460 --> 00:45:38.700
And in Q1 here, we're going to talk about some pretty interesting revenue redistribution mechanics at the platform level that will allow some of these businesses to sustain potentially, you know, without tokens at all, which is, you know, pretty exciting.

623
00:45:39.440 --> 00:45:46.240
Do you have competitors or do you see this as a pie that everybody can just share a piece of?

624
00:45:46.240 --> 00:45:49.060
So the Bitcoin space itself is absolutely enormous.

625
00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:59.300
And I think we're in this really interesting spot where so many folks are trying to view it as zero sum when it's just an absolutely monstrous space.

626
00:45:59.600 --> 00:46:03.480
We have competitors, of course, but we honestly don't pay much attention.

627
00:46:03.820 --> 00:46:05.900
We continue to run our own race.

628
00:46:05.900 --> 00:46:11.800
At the end of the day, there's more than enough for, you know, many large Bitcoin chains out there.

629
00:46:11.920 --> 00:46:16.140
We do think there will be a power law in terms of, you know, the ultimate size of different winners.

630
00:46:16.640 --> 00:46:20.740
But what we care about is actual adoption, actual usage, real protocols.

631
00:46:21.260 --> 00:46:31.520
And I think the litmus test that, you know, I have talked about, you know, in the past as well is go look at the block explorers for actual usage and activity on many of these, you know, Bitcoin scaling solutions.

632
00:46:31.520 --> 00:46:33.560
And you'll see that they're just ghost chains already.

633
00:46:33.560 --> 00:46:42.240
Right. And if you're at a ghost chain, pre-TGE and everything else, like my odds are not super high that that becomes something that has real staying power.

634
00:46:42.420 --> 00:46:52.080
But I think many of these people need to stop pitchforking each other and that sort of thing and just say, hey, how do we collaborate and, you know, cooperate, if you will, in order to build something big?

635
00:46:52.080 --> 00:46:54.460
And I think that's definitely a focus for core in 2025.

636
00:46:54.460 --> 00:46:59.280
I think many of these groups, you know, that view us as competitive, we probably view as potential partners.

637
00:46:59.280 --> 00:47:03.440
And there's just so much opportunity here to build something enormous.

638
00:47:03.440 --> 00:47:08.600
And I don't think it's like an end of one style play.

639
00:47:08.700 --> 00:47:18.700
I think it's a one of end where the entire, let's call it, you know, top 50 by most metrics in crypto will be dominated by Bitcoin scaling solutions in the next three to five years.

640
00:47:18.700 --> 00:47:28.040
So what's the difference when it comes to communicating with Web 2 users versus these DGEN Web 3 users?

641
00:47:28.800 --> 00:47:32.900
I would assume it's education and a lot of explaining.

642
00:47:32.900 --> 00:47:38.820
So I refer to myself as the chief education officer for core.

643
00:47:38.820 --> 00:47:39.100
I love that.

644
00:47:39.940 --> 00:47:43.420
Don't quote me, but that is one that I use, you know, pretty often.

645
00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:48.180
And it's not just educating Web 2 or Web 3, it's educating everyone.

646
00:47:48.680 --> 00:47:51.660
Like for the last 16 years, happy birthday, Bitcoin.

647
00:47:52.200 --> 00:47:56.180
People have forever just thought about it as like kind of a pet rock.

648
00:47:56.180 --> 00:48:01.640
And that pet rock has gone up in money or gone up in value for the last, you know, every year.

649
00:48:02.120 --> 00:48:03.440
And that's been awesome.

650
00:48:03.780 --> 00:48:06.140
But now you can use it for so much more.

651
00:48:06.400 --> 00:48:13.000
So even for some of your hardcore DGENs and that sort of thing, we're having your, you know, Bitcoin scaling one-on-one conversation.

652
00:48:13.000 --> 00:48:14.200
And that's totally cool.

653
00:48:14.660 --> 00:48:20.960
And that's how we approach, you know, kind of all these roads with the largest financial institutions in the world or a new Web 2 person or whomever.

654
00:48:21.560 --> 00:48:22.500
Here's what Bitcoin is.

655
00:48:22.580 --> 00:48:23.380
Here's why it's so special.

656
00:48:23.500 --> 00:48:24.420
Here's their value props.

657
00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:26.180
Oh, you've already got that module down.

658
00:48:26.320 --> 00:48:27.300
Okay, let's go into it, right?

659
00:48:27.560 --> 00:48:28.980
But you kind of take it step by step.

660
00:48:29.460 --> 00:48:34.880
And that's what we found out to be, you know, super, super impactful overall is meeting people where they're at.

661
00:48:35.300 --> 00:48:46.980
If we try to just bury you with like kind of the latest and greatest like, you know, Web 3 farming trends or something, you're actually going to scare away vast majority of people out there that, you know, get scared when they hear about things like yield, right?

662
00:48:47.040 --> 00:48:50.660
You have to kind of meet people where they're at and ultimately help bring them along that journey.

663
00:48:50.660 --> 00:49:05.420
And that's also why I talk about that continuum all the time about bringing people along their journey because the quickest way to destroy trust, and of course, you know, I've made this mistake as well, is if you try to bring people too far along that spectrum before they're ready.

664
00:49:05.420 --> 00:49:13.680
And that's one of the reasons that we do have products that are, you know, all along the spectrum, because you have to allow people to mature at their own pace.

665
00:49:13.680 --> 00:49:16.040
And you can, of course, try to add gas or add emphasis.

666
00:49:16.040 --> 00:49:24.120
But unless people understand you're going to wind up with upset users, and that, unfortunately, is, you know, how you wind up building something that doesn't quite work.

667
00:49:24.700 --> 00:49:34.580
Does talking to institution and talking to like a retail person, do you use different words or different logic, different reasoning, or are they all the same?

668
00:49:35.000 --> 00:49:35.720
Really depends.

669
00:49:35.720 --> 00:49:47.360
I think it's hard to be that broad, if you will, because even if you go into different financial institutions, like they have totally different mindsets and so many different educational pieces.

670
00:49:47.740 --> 00:49:50.940
We're really lucky that there's a good understanding of Bitcoin today.

671
00:49:51.480 --> 00:49:53.680
Like a lot of that hard work was done for us.

672
00:49:54.120 --> 00:49:59.560
And we can then use that as the entry part to that story and then talking about adding utility onto Bitcoin.

673
00:49:59.560 --> 00:50:02.480
And like, but it's nice to have that base layer done.

674
00:50:02.860 --> 00:50:07.360
I think if we didn't have that, it'd be a much harder conversation with a lot of these different folks.

675
00:50:07.600 --> 00:50:13.580
But with some of these actors, even though they might understand Bitcoin, they've got very different levels of sophistication when it comes to DeFi or staking.

676
00:50:13.940 --> 00:50:18.460
So we do try to approach it in very like nuts and bolts, kind of 101 pieces for everybody.

677
00:50:18.460 --> 00:50:21.600
But they, of course, have their own questions, tangents that you go down, etc.

678
00:50:21.880 --> 00:50:24.320
And we learn a lot as part of every conversation.

679
00:50:24.320 --> 00:50:27.820
So two very common questions I got for people.

680
00:50:27.820 --> 00:50:31.360
How do you know Bitcoin is not all made up?

681
00:50:31.440 --> 00:50:32.740
Because you can't see it.

682
00:50:33.240 --> 00:50:38.260
You say it's digital gold, but for gold, at least you can wear it as a jewelry.

683
00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:42.020
You can put it in chips, but you can't see Bitcoin.

684
00:50:42.920 --> 00:50:44.540
You don't even know who made Bitcoin.

685
00:50:44.540 --> 00:50:49.780
So, and I'm like, I explain it in a way like hard money.

686
00:50:50.520 --> 00:50:53.300
And that's the Michael Saylor type of explanation.

687
00:50:53.300 --> 00:50:58.380
But I want to know how you would explain or address this to newcomers.

688
00:50:58.380 --> 00:51:01.920
So this is a, I can't remember when I stopped getting this question.

689
00:51:01.920 --> 00:51:04.300
Maybe it was like 2021 or something.

690
00:51:04.720 --> 00:51:06.960
Like my family used to ask me this stuff all the time.

691
00:51:07.420 --> 00:51:07.940
Right, right.

692
00:51:08.240 --> 00:51:13.060
And you would always get like, but the U.S. dollar is like backed by the U.S. military.

693
00:51:13.320 --> 00:51:14.520
Or like, you know, whatever.

694
00:51:14.520 --> 00:51:17.480
And you'd get to these like, you know, non-pulsifiable points.

695
00:51:18.060 --> 00:51:21.440
I think the, which are like largely true in some ways.

696
00:51:21.560 --> 00:51:28.160
But I think kind of how I think about it is most things in the world have value that is hard to quantify.

697
00:51:28.160 --> 00:51:36.540
And it's really difficult, like beyond the Saylor, like economic arguments, if you will, which like a lot of people just, you know, discount.

698
00:51:36.740 --> 00:51:41.360
But I, again, could not agree more of where you have provable scarcity, everything else.

699
00:51:41.540 --> 00:51:46.980
You can just talk about things like, like paintings or other things where it's like, how do you put a value on that?

700
00:51:47.400 --> 00:51:52.060
And people will try to give you a framework and whatever, but it eventually will boil down to subjectivity.

701
00:51:52.060 --> 00:52:02.440
And I think that's the other like non-falsifiable hypothesis that you can use to like kind of end that conversation where it's like, okay, everything that has value is ultimately subjective.

702
00:52:03.000 --> 00:52:09.620
And if you can bring people to that level of reasoning, like it usually kind of kills the stump to stumper type game that people play.

703
00:52:10.080 --> 00:52:20.560
But I just got tired of it because you either understand the economics of it and you're like willing to be a good faith actor or you want to just like kind of play, you know, one of those games that's annoying.

704
00:52:20.560 --> 00:52:26.500
But yeah, that was one of my favorites was using fine art or those sorts of things as a retort.

705
00:52:26.960 --> 00:52:30.340
You know, Bitcoiners, they see Bitcoin as the store value.

706
00:52:30.700 --> 00:52:35.960
And there are people that desire more utility and they want to use core.

707
00:52:36.920 --> 00:52:49.860
Can you tell us more or take us to the challenges that you guys might have or the conversations you might have with, you know, the other side of the community?

708
00:52:49.860 --> 00:53:00.160
So I think one of the most, like kind of most early insights that we had that was really, really useful was that people think about Bitcoiners as one group.

709
00:53:00.660 --> 00:53:04.300
And that is just a very flawed assumption.

710
00:53:05.040 --> 00:53:06.920
Bitcoiners are totally heterogeneous.

711
00:53:06.920 --> 00:53:12.760
And it now is so mainstream that you've got heads of state, nation states that have Bitcoin.

712
00:53:12.760 --> 00:53:18.380
And then you have, you know, kids that are in there like, you know, I don't know, like 10 years old or something that big, right?

713
00:53:18.420 --> 00:53:22.040
Like, like, how do you view that group as like the same, right?

714
00:53:22.120 --> 00:53:24.360
And you can't is the ultimate answer.

715
00:53:24.360 --> 00:53:44.100
And that led us down this journey of building different products, different educational materials for everybody that's on that step, because that's a mistake that many of our competitors and of course we've made, you know, previous times in the past, where if you try to square peg in a round hole or treat these folks as one group, you're ultimately going to lead to pain.

716
00:53:44.100 --> 00:53:48.080
Because people have different things they're willing to do or willing to accept.

717
00:53:48.280 --> 00:53:52.420
And if you try to force them along, again, you'll wind up with upset, you know, customers.

718
00:53:52.960 --> 00:54:02.560
A very good example of this is if you look at, you know, kind of BTCFi in general, Core fully acknowledges that BTCFi is like maybe $100 billion opportunity today.

719
00:54:03.140 --> 00:54:08.360
I think BitcoinFi will become a trillion dollar opportunity not far from now, but we are not there yet.

720
00:54:08.360 --> 00:54:15.900
And I think if you look at these other groups, it's like, what if I pull all of Bitcoin over here tomorrow and just in reality, that's not going to happen.

721
00:54:16.440 --> 00:54:18.300
And we, you know, talk to people all the time.

722
00:54:18.380 --> 00:54:21.380
I would love that more than anybody else, you know, like, don't get me wrong.

723
00:54:21.540 --> 00:54:25.340
But if you live in the real world, you understand that that is just not where we're at today.

724
00:54:25.520 --> 00:54:29.540
And BitcoinFi is going to be a story not just of this cycle, but of the next cycle.

725
00:54:29.660 --> 00:54:37.780
And like maybe that's when it hits, you know, China, the truly crazy, you know, adoption scale that we're looking for and like the hundreds of millions or billions of people.

726
00:54:37.780 --> 00:54:47.400
But in terms of like addressing these different groups of the community, it's all about expectation setting, talking about how we are Bitcoiners building for Bitcoin.

727
00:54:47.760 --> 00:54:59.660
And we're actually working in the weeds to go improve kind of the potential utility of Bitcoin versus trying to take away or damage the base layer or do any of these other things they might view as parasitic, if you would.

728
00:55:00.080 --> 00:55:07.040
And we have a fairly high conversion rate as part of those conversations, provided we're having like good faith discussions, right?

729
00:55:07.040 --> 00:55:11.700
And you can have bad faith discussions with Ethereum community members, Solana community, you name it, right?

730
00:55:12.100 --> 00:55:15.000
But that's been a great eye-opening experience for us.

731
00:55:15.020 --> 00:55:18.440
And we've had these conversations with some of the largest, most famous Bitcoin holders.

732
00:55:18.940 --> 00:55:22.500
And it's just a process of, you know, kind of level setting and having good faith discussion.

733
00:55:22.940 --> 00:55:29.020
Anything exciting that is happening at core or will be happening soon?

734
00:55:29.420 --> 00:55:32.480
2025 is definitely a big year of scale for us.

735
00:55:32.480 --> 00:55:36.020
So, again, we have like 300x TPL growth and that sort of thing last year, which is awesome.

736
00:55:36.200 --> 00:55:41.180
It was really like our coming out party, something like 50x, I think, growth in daily active users.

737
00:55:41.760 --> 00:55:44.660
Next year is how do we go 10x that again, right?

738
00:55:44.700 --> 00:55:49.760
And like how do we go from not just being the biggest Bitcoin scaling chain, which we're at right now, but to one of the largest chains overall?

739
00:55:50.260 --> 00:55:52.520
And we have really ambitious goals to go do that.

740
00:55:52.520 --> 00:55:55.400
And you're going to see more and more adoption, more and more protocols.

741
00:55:55.580 --> 00:56:01.100
Like we couldn't be any more excited about what 2025 has in store, like a macro level, if you will.

742
00:56:01.700 --> 00:56:06.460
In Q1, we're super excited that the first implementation of local fee markets will go live on core.

743
00:56:06.840 --> 00:56:12.420
So if you're not spamming the network, you'll have faster, cheaper transactions, regardless of like network congestion.

744
00:56:12.620 --> 00:56:14.020
We're super fired up about that.

745
00:56:14.020 --> 00:56:23.320
We're going to do some very interesting revenue redistribution plays where if you're interacting with certain dApps or stable coins, et cetera, you'll have different economic principles applied to it.

746
00:56:23.440 --> 00:56:24.760
So we're super excited about that.

747
00:56:24.840 --> 00:56:28.440
We'll probably do a whole podcast on that at some point and go into like the different nitty-gritty and the technicals.

748
00:56:28.740 --> 00:56:38.380
But it's the idea is like how do we build the best platform, again, not just for Bitcoin, but for any builder in the space as we go approach more and more builders and have more and more people launch stuff on top of core.

749
00:56:38.380 --> 00:56:45.580
There'll be a whole host of partnerships in Q1, more custodians getting announced, different providers, different funds taking positions in dual staking.

750
00:56:46.080 --> 00:56:47.140
It should be action-packed.

751
00:56:47.340 --> 00:56:51.260
I want to know your thoughts on this cycle.

752
00:56:51.700 --> 00:57:02.360
So do you think a super cycle is coming because, you know, with Michael Saylor meeting Eric Trump, Trump is going to be in office in a few days.

753
00:57:02.360 --> 00:57:03.720
I don't know.

754
00:57:03.800 --> 00:57:08.440
People are super stoked that, you know, an omega or like a god candle is incoming.

755
00:57:09.480 --> 00:57:17.840
But on the other hand, there are people saying, hey, it's still like a four-year cycle where you go from Bitcoin and then Ethereum, big caps, small caps.

756
00:57:18.480 --> 00:57:19.340
Which one is true?

757
00:57:19.920 --> 00:57:21.240
Man, I wish I knew the answer to that.

758
00:57:22.180 --> 00:57:24.260
But again, not financial advice.

759
00:57:25.680 --> 00:57:28.800
And of course, by saying that magic phrase, like it absolves me of all things.

760
00:57:28.800 --> 00:57:39.860
But in terms of like kind of this cycle being different, I would agree with because I think we have this more or less fixed capital base in these ETFs that continues to grow, right?

761
00:57:39.880 --> 00:57:40.760
It's $100 billion now.

762
00:57:40.860 --> 00:57:41.500
It's making it grow.

763
00:57:42.060 --> 00:57:44.820
And I think it is a generally this cycle is different.

764
00:57:45.340 --> 00:57:48.900
How that impacts, is it five years now, six years super cycle?

765
00:57:49.040 --> 00:57:50.720
Like that part I think is really hard to tell.

766
00:57:50.820 --> 00:57:54.920
But I do think there is a structural shift in demand, particularly for Bitcoin, right?

767
00:57:54.920 --> 00:57:57.460
And like there's a lot of talk about nation states buying Bitcoin.

768
00:57:57.860 --> 00:58:00.560
And I know that folks at Bitcoin Magazine have been talking a lot about that.

769
00:58:00.640 --> 00:58:05.440
And I think we will see like there is a demand curve shift adjustment that's occurred in Bitcoin.

770
00:58:05.440 --> 00:58:07.840
And I think that will flow through to some other crypto assets.

771
00:58:08.140 --> 00:58:10.620
Does it do the traditional big cap, mid cap?

772
00:58:10.760 --> 00:58:12.440
Like I don't know as much.

773
00:58:12.580 --> 00:58:13.680
I wish I did.

774
00:58:14.040 --> 00:58:17.820
But I do think a lot of the former cyclical pieces will take place.

775
00:58:17.820 --> 00:58:22.860
But I could be maybe drawn out or elongated based on some of these new drivers.

776
00:58:24.200 --> 00:58:26.120
Do you see game theory playing out?

777
00:58:26.420 --> 00:58:28.200
Nation states game theory?

778
00:58:28.960 --> 00:58:37.760
You know, as U.S. started their, what is it, Bitcoin National Reserve, other countries are going to Russia.

779
00:58:38.040 --> 00:58:40.000
I'm very intrigued to see it.

780
00:58:40.400 --> 00:58:45.900
I think in general, game theory is like one of the most useful things for anybody to understand and appreciate,

781
00:58:45.900 --> 00:58:48.060
especially anybody that works in crypto.

782
00:58:48.900 --> 00:58:55.580
I do think when you see like G7 countries starting to acquire Bitcoin, like no one has any idea what that's going to do,

783
00:58:55.800 --> 00:58:58.160
like in terms of just the bid for Bitcoin.

784
00:58:59.140 --> 00:59:04.160
But there's a whole bunch of other maybe pretty negative externalities that people aren't talking about.

785
00:59:04.300 --> 00:59:06.860
Okay, do you get confiscation in some countries?

786
00:59:07.040 --> 00:59:12.180
Do you get like, you get to a lot of weird things where, hey, maybe it's not so good to get there overnight.

787
00:59:12.180 --> 00:59:17.180
And, you know, one of the questions that I got from people, you know, particularly last year was like,

788
00:59:17.420 --> 00:59:18.980
when are we going to get a million dollar Bitcoin now?

789
00:59:19.040 --> 00:59:20.460
It's like, well, I hope it's not this year.

790
00:59:20.840 --> 00:59:24.280
Because if it's this year, like, I don't know, like the world is burning around us.

791
00:59:24.380 --> 00:59:26.940
You know, like you don't want to happen too quickly, if you will.

792
00:59:26.940 --> 00:59:35.440
But I do think that it will, whether it's the U.S. doing it first or a G7 country, you will see that and it will just change the Bitcoin market forever.

793
00:59:35.940 --> 00:59:37.820
And I don't know what does that make the price?

794
00:59:37.920 --> 00:59:38.820
I couldn't tell you.

795
00:59:39.140 --> 00:59:44.420
But I think it will be like a Bitcoin shortage, very different, very different setup for us all.

796
00:59:45.060 --> 00:59:46.900
So you think it will be happening?

797
00:59:47.340 --> 00:59:48.440
I hope it happens.

798
00:59:48.440 --> 00:59:52.660
I don't know if it happens next year, the following year.

799
00:59:52.820 --> 00:59:56.520
Like, it's really hard to kind of price these things when it does occur.

800
